[0.000, "o", "\r\n---\r\nname: sams-gen-writing\r\ndescription: Draft, review, or plan AI-generated or AI-assisted web content meant to appear in Google Search. Applies Google's published policy and Search Relations guidance on value, human review, scale, disclosure, and authorship without inventing experience or ranking claims.\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Sam's Gen Writing\r\n\r\n## Goal\r\n\r\nHelp the user publish AI-assisted pages that a reader would value and that stay inside Google Search policy. The test is the page's purpose and value, not whether a model wrote it.\r\n\r\nDone means the user has a draft, review, or plan that:\r\n\r\n- adds something a reader cannot get from a generic model answer;\r\n- has every factual claim and every piece of search-visible metadata marked as checked or still to check;\r\n- names who is responsible for the page and, where readers would ask, how it was made; and\r\n- separates Google's formal policy from Search Relations commentary and from your own inference.\r\n\r\n## Select the mode\r\n\r\n- **Draft:** Write or rewrite a page with"]
[0.000, "o", " the user's material. Return the draft and its review sheet.\r\n- **Review:** Assess an existing page, draft, or set of pages. Report findings; do not rewrite unless asked.\r\n- **Plan:** Design a workflow or program that uses AI to produce many pages. Gate it on per-page value before volume.\r\n- **Question:** Answer what Google says about a specific practice, with the evidence tier for each claim.\r\n\r\nInfer the mode from the request. Ask only when a wrong guess would change what gets published.\r\n\r\n## Rules\r\n\r\n1. **Judge purpose and value, not production method.** Google's spam policy targets unoriginal pages made mainly to manipulate rankings, however they are made. AI use is neither a violation nor a defense.\r\n2. **Write for readers who would arrive directly.** Do not produce a separate version for LLMs or AI features, split pages into \"bite-sized chunks\" for machines, chase word counts, or change dates without substantive changes.\r\n3. **Add what the model cannot.** Ask the user for first-hand experience, origina"]
[0.000, "o", "l data, photos, testing, local knowledge, or expert judgment. Apply the \"anyone could have written this\" test to every section. If the user has none, say the page is a commodity summary and recommend not publishing it or merging it.\r\n4. **Never fabricate the human layer.** Do not invent experience, test results, quotes, customer reviews, statistics, credentials, author names, or headshots. Insert `[NEEDS: …]` markers for material only the user can supply. Fabricated creator profiles are deceptive under Google's guidance; decline that part and offer the honest version.\r\n5. **Require human review before publishing.** Every claim needs a source or a named human check. Review title elements, meta descriptions, structured data, and image alt text as well as body text, because they can appear in Search results.\r\n6. **Treat scale as the main risk multiplier.** Many pages from templates, feeds, scraped or stitched sources, translations, or synonym swaps need a per-page answer to \"what does this page add?\" Prefer fe"]
[0.000, "o", "wer, better pages. Recommend excluding low-value generated sections from Search.\r\n7. **Make Who and How visible.** Use real bylines where readers expect them, linked to author information. Add a short, audience-appropriate note on how automation was used where a reader might ask \"How was this created?\"\r\n8. **Apply feature-specific requirements when they apply.** For Google Merchant Center, AI-generated images need IPTC `DigitalSourceType` `TrainedAlgorithmicMedia`, and AI-generated product titles and descriptions must be labeled separately. Validate structured data against Google's guidelines.\r\n9. **Use AI where it helps the writer.** Brainstorming, outlines, a first draft to react against, and wording variations are reasonable uses. The human rewrite carries the voice, judgment, and experience.\r\n\r\n## Evidence\r\n\r\nRead [Google policy](references/google-policy.md) for formal requirements and [Search Off the Record findings](references/search-off-the-record.md) for attributed commentary and history.\r\n\r\n- Rank ev"]
[0.000, "o", "idence: formal Search Central documentation, then Search Relations statements on the podcast, then guests, then practitioners. Label the tier when it matters.\r\n- Re-check the formal pages when the answer depends on current policy and you have web access. Report the date checked; if you cannot check, give the reference date.\r\n- Do not promise rankings, traffic, or penalty outcomes. Google does not publish a ranking effect for disclosure, bylines, or E-E-A-T, and raters' guidelines do not directly affect ranking.\r\n- Do not claim AI-detection results or that Google \"detects AI.\" Google's documents talk about value, effort, originality, and purpose.\r\n- Mark generalization from podcast remarks or practitioner anecdotes as inference. Treat 2021–2022 remarks requiring human supervision as superseded by the February 2023 guidance unless the user asks about history.\r\n- This skill covers Google Search. Do not assume the same rules for other search engines or AI assistants.\r\n\r\n## Output\r\n\r\nMatch the mode and the user'"]
[0.000, "o", "s format. Include only what helps:\r\n\r\n- **Draft:** the page; then a review sheet listing `[NEEDS: …]` gaps, claims to verify with suggested sources, metadata to check, the byline and How note, and any scale or feature-specific requirement.\r\n- **Review:** findings ordered by severity: policy risk (spam policies, deception), then reader value, then trust signals, then polish. Each finding gives the evidence, the rule, its source tier, and a concrete fix.\r\n- **Plan:** the per-page value gate, human review step, sampling rate for audits, exclusion rule for low-value pages, and how disclosure and authorship work.\r\n- **Question:** a short answer, then the supporting evidence with tiers and dates.\r\n\r\nEnd with a status:\r\n\r\n- `ready-for-human-review`: no known gaps beyond final human checks;\r\n- `needs-user-material`: `[NEEDS: …]` gaps remain;\r\n- `do-not-publish-as-is`: policy risk or no added value; or\r\n- `policy-unverified`: the answer depends on current pages you could not check.\r\n# Google policy on AI-generated"]
[0.000, "o", " content\r\n\r\nFormal Google Search Central documentation, paraphrased. Pages checked 2026-10-07; each entry gives the page's own last-updated date. Re-check before relying on any requirement for a consequential decision.\r\n\r\n## Guidance on using generative AI content\r\n\r\nSource: [Google Search's guidance on using generative AI content](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/using-gen-ai-content) (page updated 2026-10-01).\r\n\r\n- Generative AI can help research a topic and add structure to original content.\r\n- AI-assisted content must meet Search Essentials and the spam policies.\r\n- Generating many pages without adding user value may violate the scaled content abuse policy.\r\n- The Search Quality Rater Guidelines cover scaled content abuse (section 4.6.5) and main content made with little effort, originality, or added value (section 4.6.6). Rater guidelines do not directly influence ranking.\r\n- Models predict likely text and can produce inaccuracies, so fact-check and review content before publishing."]
[0.000, "o", " This includes title elements, meta descriptions, structured data, and image alt text.\r\n- Follow general and feature-specific structured data guidelines and validate markup.\r\n- Give readers context on how content was created, in a way that suits the audience, and add image metadata where relevant.\r\n- Google Merchant Center: AI-generated images need IPTC `DigitalSourceType` set to `TrainedAlgorithmicMedia`. AI-generated product data such as titles and descriptions must be specified separately and labeled as AI-generated.\r\n\r\n## Creating helpful, reliable, people-first content\r\n\r\nSource: [Creating helpful, reliable, people-first content](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content) (page updated 2026-10-05).\r\n\r\n- **Who:** Readers should be able to tell who made the content. Use bylines where readers expect them, linked to author information. Fabricated creator profiles, including AI-generated headshots, invented names, or false credentials, are deceptive.\r\n- **How:** Explainin"]
[0.000, "o", "g how content was produced, including automated or AI-assisted production, builds trust. Disclose where a reader might wonder how it was created, and consider explaining why automation helped.\r\n- **Why:** Google calls this the most important question. Content should exist mainly to help people. Using automation mainly to manipulate rankings violates the spam policies.\r\n- Self-assessment questions ask whether content offers original information, research, or analysis; first-hand expertise; review by someone who knows the topic; and insight beyond the obvious.\r\n- Warning signs of search-engine-first content include mainly summarizing others without adding value, extensive automation across many topics, changing dates without substantive change, entering a niche only for traffic, and writing to a word count. Google says it has no preferred word count.\r\n- E-E-A-T means experience, expertise, authoritativeness, and trustworthiness. Trust matters most. E-E-A-T is not a specific ranking factor; systems weigh it more"]
[0.000, "o", " for \"Your Money or Your Life\" topics.\r\n\r\n## Spam policies\r\n\r\nSource: [Spam policies for Google web search](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies) (page updated 2026-08-28).\r\n\r\n**Scaled content abuse:** many pages generated mainly to manipulate rankings rather than help users, usually unoriginal and low-value, no matter how they are created. Listed examples include:\r\n\r\n- generative AI or similar tools producing many pages without user value;\r\n- scraping feeds, search results, or other content into many pages, including through synonymizing, translating, or other obfuscation;\r\n- stitching content from several pages without adding value;\r\n- creating multiple sites to hide the scale; and\r\n- pages that make little sense to readers but contain search keywords.\r\n\r\nThe page advises excluding such content from Search if a site hosts it.\r\n\r\n**Site reputation policy:** third-party content published mainly to exploit a host site's ranking signals. Third-party content alone is not a violation"]
[0.000, "o", "; editorial columns, syndication, user-generated content, and ordinary advertorials are listed as acceptable.\r\n\r\n**Thin affiliation:** affiliate pages copying merchant descriptions without original content or added value. Good affiliate pages add price information, original reviews, testing, navigation, or comparisons.\r\n\r\n## History\r\n\r\nGoogle published its general AI-content stance in [Google Search's guidance about AI-generated content](https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2023/02/google-search-and-ai-content) (Search Central Blog, February 2023). That post framed AI content under the existing helpful-content approach. Earlier public remarks, including a widely discussed 2022 statement that auto-generated content was against guidelines ([Hacker News discussion, April 2022](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=30969620)), predate it. The [March 2024 core update and spam policies post](https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2024/03/core-update-spam-policies) introduced the scaled content abuse policy, "]
[0.000, "o", "which broadened the earlier policy on spammy automatically generated content. The blog bodies could not be retrieved on 2026-10-07, so the March 2024 change is from prior knowledge, not a fresh read; the current requirements above were read directly.\r\n# Search Off the Record findings on AI content\r\n\r\nParaphrased from Google's published transcripts of the Search Off the Record podcast. Remarks by Search Relations staff are commentary, not formal policy; guest remarks carry less weight still. Transcripts were published by Google and have not been verified against audio. Reviewed 2026-10-07 across 111 available transcripts; the December 2025 episode \"Thoughts on SEO & SEO for AI, part 1\" had no transcript available and is not covered.\r\n\r\nUse these to explain why the formal rules exist and how Googlers think about AI-assisted writing. When a podcast remark and current documentation differ, follow the documentation.\r\n\r\n## Current direction (2023–2026)\r\n\r\n**Don't make machine-shaped content.** Danny Sullivan said"]
[0.000, "o", " Google does not want people splitting content into bite-sized chunks for LLMs or keeping one version for LLMs and another for people. Tactics tuned to a current system may stop working as systems improve toward rewarding content written for humans. For a local business, he suggested effort goes further on happy customers and reviews than on restructuring pages for LLMs. — [SEO, AIO, GEO, your site, & third-party support to optimize for LLMs](https://search-off-the-record.libsyn.com/seo-aio-geo-your-site-third-party-support-to-optimize-for-llms), 2026-01-08.\r\n\r\n**Generic AI sites can lose indexing.** John Mueller said that when Google's systems have serious concerns about a site's overall quality, they crawl and index less. He gave the example of a mostly AI-generated site that once worked but where readers can tell nothing unique is offered, and said not all AI-generated content is bad. He framed the fix as stepping back to assess quality rather than chasing a technical cause. — [How to read the Indexing"]
[0.000, "o", " Report](https://search-off-the-record.libsyn.com/how-to-read-the-indexing-report), 2026-07-16.\r\n\r\n**Authentic content is the durable bet.** Discussing ranking systems, John Mueller said authentic content made for a known audience is the way forward, and that AI content will have useful places while the foundation remains pointing people to authentic information. Danny Sullivan said a ranking-system update should not change what a creator does if they are already making helpful content. — [Let's talk ranking updates](https://search-off-the-record.libsyn.com/help-the-algorithm-has-been-updated), 2023-08-22.\r\n\r\n**Use AI to start, then rewrite.** Martin Splitt described using generative AI to get past the blank page, then rejecting and reworking the draft into what he meant to say. He hoped creators would learn to use AI well rather than flood the web with it. — [2023 year in review](https://search-off-the-record.libsyn.com/2023-year-in-review), 2023-12-21.\r\n\r\n**The problem is how it's used.** Gary Illyes sa"]
[0.000, "o", "id generative tools are not inherently bad and can help with structure and wording under deadline; John Mueller mentioned brainstorming. — [Which aspect of my site should I focus on?](https://search-off-the-record.libsyn.com/which-aspect-of-my-site-should-i-focus-on), 2023-11-21.\r\n\r\n**Always fact-check.** In an episode testing Gemini-written social posts, Gary Illyes said his main concern with generative interfaces is factuality and that output always needs fact-checking. He noted grounding against search results does not guarantee correctness. — [Gemini for Social Posts: Game Edition](https://search-off-the-record.libsyn.com/gemini-for-social-posts-game-edition), 2024-04-25.\r\n\r\n## Practitioner perspective (guest)\r\n\r\n**Edited AI can work; churn does not.** Guest Jennifer Slegg said she had seen good results when a skilled copywriter reworked AI drafts, but that mass-publishing thousands of generated articles is not the way to go. — [From journalism to SEO with Jennifer Slegg](https://search-off-the-reco"]
[0.000, "o", "rd.libsyn.com/from-journalism-to-seo-with-jennifer-slegg), 2022-11-30.\r\n\r\n## Superseded framing\r\n\r\n**2021: human supervision as the line.** Gary Illyes said Google's stance then was that machine-generated content without human supervision was unwanted in Search, while reviewed content was fine. John Mueller raised the risk of fluent but wrong output. — [The future of SEO](https://search-off-the-record.libsyn.com/the-future-of-seo), 2021-11-11.\r\n\r\nThis predates the February 2023 guidance, which judges content by value and purpose rather than by production method. Keep the fact-checking concern; do not present \"a human looked at it\" as sufficient.\r\n\r\n## Patterns\r\n\r\n- Every current source treats value to readers as the test and scale without value as the risk. This is consistent across formal docs and commentary.\r\n- Googlers describe AI as a writing aid (drafts, structure, brainstorming) and consistently pair it with human rewriting and fact-checking.\r\n- No episode reviewed gives a ranking benefit for AI discl"]
[0.000, "o", "osure or a method for detecting AI text. Inference: avoid claiming either.\r\n- Coverage is uneven. Most remarks are brief asides in episodes about other topics, and no full episode on AI-written content was available.\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\r\n"]
[3.508, "o", "\u001b[1mweb search:\u001b[0m \r\n"]
[1.282, "o", "\u001b[1mweb search:\u001b[0m https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/using-gen-ai-content\r\n"]
[3.435, "o", "\u001b[1mweb search:\u001b[0m \r\n"]
